// ============================================================================
// Resolution · TYPE RESOLUTION  (name -> type, client + lib)
//
// Resolves a type reference to a concrete type declaration, scoped by the
// referencing file's imports / same-file types / wildcard imports / same-package
// visibility, the implicit java.lang, and — for dotted references — nested (line-
// range) and inline-FQN resolution. Unified over client (java_*) and library
// (lib_*) with a provenance tag, so a client reference resolves to whichever side
// declares the type (this is where the client->external TYPE boundary appears).
//
// Uses raw imported facts (java_type_reference / java_import / java_type / lib_type)
// rather than projections — resolution does multi-way joins, and raw facts avoid
// the two-derived-projection join issue.
//
// Two resolution regimes, split by the ref_unqualified guard:
//   UNQUALIFIED refs (typeName == completeTypeName) use the simple-name file-scoped
//     rules. Enforced ordering: single-type import (Case 1) and same-file (Case 2)
//     each suppress the rest; java.lang is a true last resort (!type_ref_file_resolved).
//     Same-package/default-package (Case 4) RANKS ABOVE wildcard import (Case 3), per
//     JLS 6.5.5.1: a same-package type shadows on-demand imports. Enforced via the
//     !type_has_same_pkg_match guard on Case 3, so a name visible via both resolves to
//     the same-package type alone (not both). Single-type import (Case 1) still
//     outranks same-package.
//   QUALIFIED refs (typeName != completeTypeName, e.g. Map.Entry, java.util.Date)
//     skip the simple-name rules entirely (else the bare inner name spuriously
//     matches a same-named client type) and resolve via the completeTypeName-keyed
//     rules below: a recursive member-walk (client) / line-range hop (lib), + inline-FQN.
//
// Nested qualified names: the parser names a nested type by its full chain
// (pkg.Outer.Inner), so an import path or an inline FQN equals the qualifiedName of a
// nested type just as it does for a top-level one. A library IR parsed before that
// change still carries the FLATTENED form (pkg.Inner, the outer dropped), so every
// lib-side rule keeps the line-range hop that never depended on the string, and the
// TOP_LEVEL filters on the exact-name rules stay: they cost nothing on the new form and
// keep a flattened `java.util.Arrays.ArrayList` from colliding with `java.util.ArrayList`.
//
// Column maps (0-indexed):
//   java_type_reference (18): 2 typeRegistryLinkHash(owning type) 8 typeName
//     9 completeTypeName 17 typeReferenceUniqueHash
//   java_import (11): 0 importKind 1 importedPath 2 packageOrTypeName 3 simpleName 4 filePath
//   java_type / lib_type (14): 0 name 1 qualifiedName 6 typePlacement 7 filePath 13 hash
// ============================================================================

// ── ref_file(RefHash, TypeName, FilePath) ───────────────────────────────────
// Each client type-reference: its simple name and the file it appears in (the file
// of the type that owns the reference).
ref_file(ref, typeName, fp) :- java_type_reference(_, _, ownerType, _, _, _, _, _, typeName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, ownerType).

// ── helpers for precedence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
type_has_explicit_import(name, fp) :- java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", _, _, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _).
type_has_same_file_type(name, fp) :- java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _).

// ── type_resolves_in_file(Prov, Name, FilePath, TypeHash) ───────────────────
// Case 1 — single-type import (import a.b.C): the file imports the FQN; match a
// client or lib type whose qualifiedName equals the imported path. The client side
// matches any placement: a nested type's qualifiedName is its full chain, so
// `import a.b.Outer.Inner` equals it directly. The lib side is restricted to
// TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT (col 6) because a library IR may still carry flattened nested
// names, under which a private nested type registered as java.util.ArrayList
// (Arrays.ArrayList) collides with the real top-level type; nested library imports
// resolve via Case 1b, which never depended on the string.
type_resolves_in_file("client", name, fp, t) :- java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", imp, _, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    java_type(name, imp, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
type_resolves_in_file("lib", name, fp, t) :- java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", imp, _, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    lib_type(name, imp, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// Case 1b — single-type import of a NESTED type (import a.b.Outer.Inner): the
// imported simple name (Inner) is then written unqualified. The import's
// packageOrTypeName (a.b.Outer) names the enclosing type; locate the imported name
// declared inside it by line-range containment. This is the only path for a library
// IR whose nested names are flattened (Map.Entry registered as java.util.Entry, so
// importedPath != qualifiedName); on the client it agrees with Case 1. Top-level
// imports don't match here (their packageOrTypeName is a package, not a type).
type_resolves_in_file("client", name, fp, th) :- java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", _, outerQName, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    java_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    java_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    java_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe.
type_resolves_in_file("lib", name, fp, th) :- java_import("SINGLE_TYPE", _, outerQName, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    lib_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    lib_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    lib_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe.

// Case 1c — static import of a NESTED TYPE (import static a.b.Outer.Inner): a static
// import names any static member of Outer, and a static nested type is one (JLS
// 7.5.3). The parser records it as SINGLE_STATIC with simpleName Inner, like a
// static method import, so Case 1 (SINGLE_TYPE) never sees it. Client: the imported
// path equals the nested type's qualifiedName. Lib: locate Inner inside the type
// named by packageOrTypeName by line range, as Case 1b does.
type_resolves_in_file("client", name, fp, t) :- java_import("SINGLE_STATIC", imp, _, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    java_type(name, imp, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
type_resolves_in_file("lib", name, fp, th) :- java_import("SINGLE_STATIC", _, outerQName, name, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    lib_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    lib_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    lib_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe.

// Case 2 — same file (no import needed): a type declared in the same file as the
// reference. Suppressed when an explicit import for the same name wins.
type_resolves_same_file("client", name, fp, t) :- java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp).
type_resolves_in_file(prov, name, fp, t) :- type_resolves_same_file(prov, name, fp, t).

// type_has_same_pkg_match(Name, FilePath) — a TOP_LEVEL type of this simple name is
// visible in the referencing file's OWN package (i.e. Case 4 resolves it). Per JLS
// 6.5.5.1 a same-package type shadows on-demand (wildcard) imports, so Case 3 below
// must yield to it. Equi-join on pkg (via file_pkg/type_pkg) — no cross product.
type_has_same_pkg_match(name, fp) :- file_pkg(fp, pkg),
    type_pkg(th, pkg),
    java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, th).

// Case 3 — wildcard import (import a.b.*): a TOP_LEVEL type in the wildcarded
// package. Suppressed when a single-type import, a same-file type, OR a same-package
// type wins (the last enforces JLS same-package > on-demand precedence — without it
// wildcard and same-package are peers and a name in both over-resolves).
//
// PERFORMANCE: same trap the Case 4 comment below describes, and it must be avoided
// here too. The direct form qname = cat(pkg, cat(".", name)) makes the join
// key a function of BOTH the import's pkg and the type's name, so it cannot be hash-
// indexed — it degrades to (wildcard imports) × (all types), constructing a string per
// pair (tens of millions of interned concatenations against the full library). Instead precompute
// the import's package-with-dot once (import_pkg_dot) and EQUI-JOIN it on pkgDot against
// each type's precomputed package (type_pkg / lib_type_pkg, which already carry the dot).
import_pkg_dot(fp, pkgDot) :- java_import("TYPE_ON_DEMAND", _, pkg, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    pkgDot = cat(pkg, ".").
type_resolves_below_same_file("client", name, fp, t) :- import_pkg_dot(fp, pkgDot),
    type_pkg(t, pkgDot),
    java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp),
    !type_has_same_pkg_match(name, fp).
type_resolves_below_same_file("lib", name, fp, t) :- import_pkg_dot(fp, pkgDot),
    lib_type_pkg(t, pkgDot),
    lib_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp),
    !type_has_same_pkg_match(name, fp).

// Case 4 — same-package visibility (no import): a type in the same package as the
// referencing file is visible by simple name.
//
// PERFORMANCE: the package must be precomputed, then joined as an EQUI-JOIN on a
// shared pkg variable. A direct form — deriving the prefix on the source side and
// matching tq = cat(prefix, target) against the target's qualifiedName — makes
// the join key a function of BOTH sides, which cannot be hash-indexed; it degrades
// to a java_type × java_type nested-loop cross product (types^2, tens of millions of
// substr/cat/string-compares — minutes on a real client). Computing each type's
// package once (linear) and equi-joining on pkg is hash-indexed and ~constant per
// type.
//
// type_pkg(TypeHash, Package) — the package prefix of a type, computed once.
// Top-level packaged types: qualifiedName minus the trailing simpleName. Top-level
// default-package types (qualifiedName == simpleName): the empty package "". A
// NESTED type takes the package of the top-level type it sits in (line-range
// containment): its own qualifiedName minus its name is `pkg.Outer.`, which is not
// a package.
type_pkg(t, pkg) :- java_type(name, qname, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    plen = strlen(qname) - strlen(name),
    plen > 0,
    pkg = substr(qname, 0, plen).
type_pkg(t, "") :- java_type(name, name, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
type_pkg(t, pkg) :- type_in_type("client", t, top),
    java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, top),
    type_pkg(top, pkg).

// file_pkg(FilePath, Package) — a file's package, via any top-level type declared in
// it (all types in a compilation unit share one package).
file_pkg(fp, pkg) :- java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", fp, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    type_pkg(t, pkg).

// Same-package (and default-package) resolution as an equi-join on pkg: a TOP_LEVEL
// target whose package equals the referencing file's package. Suppressed when a
// single-type import or a same-file type wins (same-file is Case 2). Client-only:
// a client reference never resolves to a library type by same-package.
type_resolves_below_same_file("client", target, fp, th) :- file_pkg(fp, pkg),
    type_pkg(th, pkg),
    java_type(target, _, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, th),
    !type_has_explicit_import(target, fp).

// Case 3b — type-member wildcard (import a.b.Outer.*): a wildcard whose
// packageOrTypeName is a TYPE (not a package) brings that type's nested types into
// scope by simple name. Distinguished from Case 3 by packageOrTypeName resolving to
// a type; the nested member is located by line-range containment, which holds for a
// library IR whose nested names are flattened as well as for the client. Suppressed
// when a single-type import or a same-file type wins.
type_resolves_below_same_file("client", name, fp, th) :- java_import("TYPE_ON_DEMAND", _, outerQName, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    java_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    java_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    java_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe,
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp).
type_resolves_below_same_file("lib", name, fp, th) :- java_import("TYPE_ON_DEMAND", _, outerQName, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    lib_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    lib_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    lib_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe,
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp).

// Case 3c — static on-demand import (import static a.b.Outer.*): brings Outer's static
// members into scope by simple name, and its static nested types are among them
// (JLS 7.5.4). Same shape as Case 3b, keyed on the STATIC_ON_DEMAND kind.
type_resolves_below_same_file("client", name, fp, th) :- java_import("STATIC_ON_DEMAND", _, outerQName, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    java_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    java_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    java_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe,
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp).
type_resolves_below_same_file("lib", name, fp, th) :- java_import("STATIC_ON_DEMAND", _, outerQName, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    lib_type(_, outerQName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, outer),
    lib_type_lines(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, is, ie, _, _, th),
    lib_type_lines(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, tfp, _, os, oe, _, _, outer),
    is > os,
    ie <= oe,
    !type_has_explicit_import(name, fp).

// Everything below the same-file case yields to a same-file type of the name. Kept as its
// own relation because a supertype in a class header cannot see that class's own member
// types (below), and there the same-file type that suppressed these may not be visible.
type_resolves_in_file(prov, name, fp, t) :- type_resolves_below_same_file(prov, name, fp, t),
    !type_has_same_file_type(name, fp).

// ── type_resolves_java_lang(Prov, Name, TypeHash) ───────────────────────────
// Implicit java.lang.* (String, Integer, …) — available to any file, no import.
type_resolves_java_lang("lib", name, t) :- lib_type(name, qname, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    qname = cat("java.lang.", name).

// ── type_ref_resolves(Prov, RefHash, TypeHash) ──────────────────────────────
// A type reference resolved to its type. File-scoped resolution first; the
// implicit java.lang is a fallback only when nothing file-scoped matched (so a
// same-package/imported type of the same simple name wins over java.lang).
// A reference whose simple typeName equals its completeTypeName — i.e. written
// WITHOUT a dotted qualifier. Only unqualified refs may use the simple-name
// file-scoped rules; a qualified ref (Map.Entry, javax.net.ssl.SSLSocketFactory)
// must resolve solely via the qualified/FQN rules below, otherwise its bare simple
// name (Entry, SSLSocketFactory) also matches a same-named client type via
// same-file/same-package/wildcard and produces a spurious second resolution.
ref_unqualified(ref) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, tn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref).

type_ref_file_resolved(ref) :- ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    !header_hides_own_member(ref),
    type_resolves_in_file(_, name, fp, _).
type_ref_file_resolved(ref) :- header_resolves(_, ref, _).

type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t) :- ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    !header_hides_own_member(ref),
    type_resolves_in_file(prov, name, fp, t).
type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t) :- header_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── A class header cannot see the class's own member types (JLS 6.3) ─────────
// The scope of a member type is the BODY of the type that declares it, and the
// extends / implements clause is not part of the body. So in
//     class DiffLike implements Builder<String> { static class Builder { … } }
// the header's `Builder` is the top-level (imported / same-package) Builder, never
// DiffLike.Builder, though both sit in the same file. Case 2 matches any type in the
// file by simple name, so it took the nested one and, by suppressing every case below
// it, lost the real supertype: the implements edge vanished, and with it the override
// and the dispatch through the interface (#1244). A type nested inside the one whose
// header holds the reference is excluded; if no other same-file type of that name is
// left, the cases below same-file answer as they would for a file without it. A class
// cannot extend its own member (cyclic inheritance), so nothing correct is dropped.
header_ref(ref, owner) :- java_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    owner != "".
header_ref(ref, owner) :- java_type_reference(_, "IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE", owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    owner != "".
header_own_member(ref, t) :- header_ref(ref, owner),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    type_resolves_same_file(_, name, fp, t),
    type_in_type("client", t, owner).
header_hides_own_member(ref) :- header_own_member(ref, _).
header_sees_same_file(ref) :- header_hides_own_member(ref),
    header_ref(ref, owner),
    ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    type_resolves_same_file(_, name, fp, t),
    !type_in_type("client", t, owner).
header_resolves(prov, ref, t) :- header_hides_own_member(ref),
    ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    type_resolves_in_file(prov, name, fp, t),
    !header_own_member(ref, t).
header_resolves(prov, ref, t) :- header_hides_own_member(ref),
    !header_sees_same_file(ref),
    ref_file(ref, name, fp),
    type_resolves_below_same_file(prov, name, fp, t).

type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t) :- ref_file(ref, name, _),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    !type_ref_file_resolved(ref),
    !type_ref_member_declared(ref),
    type_resolves_java_lang(prov, name, t).

// ── Member type in scope through nesting or inheritance (JLS 6.3, 8.5) ─────────
// An unqualified name written inside a type is in scope when it is a member type of that
// type or of any type enclosing it, DECLARED there or INHERITED from a supertype:
// inside `class HttpConnection implements Connection`, the bare `Method` is
// Connection.Method, though no import names it and no same-file or same-package type
// carries it. The file-scoped rules cannot see it; member_type (below) can, since it walks
// type_direct_parent and type_ancestor. Applied only when nothing file-scoped resolved the
// name, so an import or a same-file declaration keeps precedence (the shadowing a JLS
// compiler applies the other way round is rare enough that the cheaper order is kept), and
// before the implicit java.lang, which a member type shadows.
ref_scope_type(ref, owner) :- java_type_reference(_, _, owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    owner != "",
    !header_ref(ref, owner).
// A header reference starts one level out: the owner's own member types are not in
// scope there (see header_ref above), those of the types enclosing it are.
ref_scope_type(ref, outer) :- header_ref(ref, owner),
    type_in_type("client", owner, outer).
ref_scope_type(ref, outer) :- ref_scope_type(ref, inner),
    type_in_type("client", inner, outer).
type_ref_resolves("client", ref, inner) :- ref_file(ref, name, _),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    !type_ref_file_resolved(ref),
    ref_scope_type(ref, scope),
    member_type(scope, name, inner).
// The java.lang guard below may not read member_type: that walks type_ancestor, which is
// derived from type_ref_resolves, and a negation over it would be a cycle. A member type
// DECLARED in an enclosing type (line-range nesting, base facts only) is enough to shadow
// java.lang; an inherited one that also shadows a java.lang name is left to over-resolve.
type_ref_member_declared(ref) :- ref_file(ref, name, _),
    ref_unqualified(ref),
    ref_scope_type(ref, scope),
    type_direct_parent("client", inner, scope),
    java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, inner).

// ── Qualified / nested resolution (completeTypeName-keyed) ───────────────────
// A reference written with a dotted qualifier — completeTypeName differs from the
// simple typeName (e.g. "Map.Entry", "Outer.Inner"). The simple-name file-scoped
// rules above cannot resolve these (they key on the bare inner name), so qualified
// refs are matched here, keyed on completeTypeName. TWO distinct mechanisms:
//   CLIENT (member_chain / nested_fqn_path, immediately below): a RECURSIVE member-
//     type walk to arbitrary depth (A.B.C…), following BOTH physical nesting
//     (type_direct_parent) AND inheritance (type_ancestor). So it resolves deep
//     nesting and inherited nested types (Sub.Inner, where Inner is declared in a
//     supertype — possibly a different file). Uses type_direct_parent + type_ancestor
//     + java_type; NO *_type_lines, and NO same-file requirement.
//   LIB (further below): the older single leftmost-qualifier hop + line-range
//     containment over lib_type_lines — the inner type nested inside the resolved
//     qualifier in the same file. This never reads the nested qualifiedName, so it
//     disambiguates a library IR that still flattens it (every X.Entry registered as
//     "java.util.Entry"; only Map's Entry sits inside Map's line span).
//
// java_type_reference (18): 2 owner  8 typeName  9 completeTypeName  17 refHash
// java_type (14): 7 filePath  13 hash
// *_type_lines (14; 9,10 int): 0 name  7 filePath  9 startLine  10 endLine  13 hash
//   (used only by the LIB rule)

// Client nested — recursive, handles arbitrary depth (A.B.C…) AND inherited nested
// types (Sub.Inner where Inner is declared in Sub's supertype). Rather than a clause
// per shape, resolve the leftmost qualifier, then walk member-type segments.
//
// member_type(Container, Name, Inner): Inner is a nested type of Container by simple
// Name — declared directly inside it, OR inherited (declared inside an ancestor).
// Nested types are inherited just like methods/fields, so a subtype exposes its
// supertypes' nested types (Sub.Inner). Uses type_direct_parent (line-range nesting)
// + type_ancestor (inheritance).
member_type(container, name, inner) :- type_direct_parent(_, inner, container),
    java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, inner).
member_type(container, name, inner) :- type_ancestor(container, anc),
    type_direct_parent(_, inner, anc),
    java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, inner).

// member_chain(Anchor, DottedName, Inner, Depth): a dotted name built by walking
// member-type segments DOWN from an anchor container, keyed by the anchor's SIMPLE
// name (A -> A.B -> A.B.C ; Sub -> Sub.B -> Sub.B.C).
//
// GLOBAL — computed ONCE per anchor, NOT per file. The earlier form threaded the
// file through the recursion, so a container visible in N files (e.g. a same-package
// outer class seen by every file in its package) had its whole member walk redone N
// times — the file x container blow-up. Here the walk is file-independent; the file
// scope is applied once at the match below, as a cheap equi-join. This is the same
// precompute-then-equi-join move as the same-package (Case 4) fix.
//
// Depth (4th arg) is capped at 5 purely as a safety valve against a pathological
// self-referential nesting/inheritance cycle. Because the walk is global, depth no
// longer multiplies cost across files, so the cap can be generous.
// ── demanded_qname(CompleteName) — qualified names actually written as references
// (completeTypeName != typeName). The member-type walks below build dotted names ONLY
// to equality-match one of these; because member_type includes INHERITED nested types,
// a large reached slice makes the walk fan combinatorially, minting a dotted string per
// nested path to depth 5 — the symbol-table blow-up. Gating the walk to paths that are a
// PREFIX of some demanded name collapses "all nested paths of all types" to "the names
// actually referenced". dotted is sliced from the demanded name (substr), not cat-
// constructed, so the only strings interned are prefixes of demanded names — a handful.
demanded_qname(ctn) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    ctn != tn.

// Base — an anchor whose SIMPLE name leads some demanded name (name followed by a dot).
member_chain(anchor, name, anchor, 1) :- java_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, anchor),
    member_type(anchor, _, _),
    demanded_qname(ctn),
    nl = strlen(name),
    nl < strlen(ctn),
    substr(ctn, 0, nl) = name,
    substr(ctn, (nl + 1-1), 1) = ".".
// Step — extend by seg only when prefix.seg is a prefix of a demanded name; dotted
// is that prefix sliced from the demanded name, so paths going nowhere mint nothing. The
// length guard (prefix + "." + seg fits in ctn) keeps every substr start in range.
member_chain(anchor, dotted, inner, dn) :- member_chain(anchor, prefix, mid, d),
    d < 5,
    member_type(mid, seg, inner),
    demanded_qname(ctn),
    pl = strlen(prefix),
    sl = strlen(seg),
    pl + 1 + sl <= strlen(ctn),
    substr(ctn, 0, pl) = prefix,
    substr(ctn, (pl + 1-1), 1) = ".",
    substr(ctn, (pl + 2-1), sl) = seg,
    dotted = substr(ctn, 0, pl + 1 + sl),
    dn = d + 1.

// A qualified client reference resolves when its completeTypeName matches a chain
// whose anchor's simple name resolves in the referencing file. File scope is a
// single equi-join here — not threaded through the walk.
type_ref_resolves("client", ref, inner) :- java_type_reference(_, _, owner, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    member_chain(anchor, ctn, inner, _),
    java_type(anchorName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, anchor),
    java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, owner),
    type_resolves_in_file("client", anchorName, fp, anchor).

// FQN-anchored nested — completeTypeName is a fully-qualified path whose leftmost
// segment is a PACKAGE, not a file-resolvable simple name (e.g.
// "a.b.c.Outer.Mid.Inner"). The scoped walk above can't anchor it, so anchor
// instead on a container type's own qualifiedName and walk member segments. Scope-
// independent — an FQN reference needs no import.
//   Base — a container type keyed by its full qualifiedName.
//   Step — extend by one member-type segment (shares member_type with the walk above).
// 3rd arg = member-walk depth, capped at 5 (same runaway guard as above).
nested_fqn_path(t, qname, 1) :- java_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    member_type(t, _, _),
    demanded_qname(ctn),
    ql = strlen(qname),
    ql < strlen(ctn),
    substr(ctn, 0, ql) = qname,
    substr(ctn, (ql + 1-1), 1) = ".".
nested_fqn_path(inner, dotted, dn) :- nested_fqn_path(container, prefix, d),
    d < 5,
    member_type(container, seg, inner),
    demanded_qname(ctn),
    pl = strlen(prefix),
    sl = strlen(seg),
    pl + 1 + sl <= strlen(ctn),
    substr(ctn, 0, pl) = prefix,
    substr(ctn, (pl + 1-1), 1) = ".",
    substr(ctn, (pl + 2-1), sl) = seg,
    dotted = substr(ctn, 0, pl + 1 + sl),
    dn = d + 1.

type_ref_resolves("client", ref, resolved) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    nested_fqn_path(resolved, ctn, _).

// Library nested (Map.Entry, Outer.Mid.Inner: the reference sits in client code, but
// the qualifier and the inner type are library types — the client->external boundary
// at a nested type). Mirrors the client recursive walk so library nesting resolves to
// arbitrary depth, not just two segments.
//
// lib_member_type is DIRECT nesting only (no inherited-nested clause): the client's
// inherited clause walks type_ancestor and, over the full JDK inheritance lattice,
// would branch enormously. A library type inheriting a supertype's nested type and
// naming it by bare simple name is rare; direct nesting covers Outer.Mid.Inner.
lib_member_type(container, name, inner) :- type_direct_parent("lib", inner, container),
    lib_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, inner).

// lib_member_chain / lib_nested_fqn: the lib twins of member_chain / nested_fqn_path
// (global walk, depth-capped at 5), one simple-name-anchored, one qualifiedName-
// anchored.
lib_member_chain(anchor, name, anchor, 1) :- lib_type(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, anchor),
    lib_member_type(anchor, _, _).
lib_member_chain(anchor, dotted, inner, dn) :- lib_member_chain(anchor, prefix, mid, d),
    d < 5,
    lib_member_type(mid, seg, inner),
    dotted = cat(prefix, cat(".", seg)),
    dn = d + 1.

lib_nested_fqn(t, qname, 1) :- lib_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t),
    lib_member_type(t, _, _).
lib_nested_fqn(inner, dotted, dn) :- lib_nested_fqn(container, prefix, d),
    d < 5,
    lib_member_type(container, seg, inner),
    dotted = cat(prefix, cat(".", seg)),
    dn = d + 1.

// Simple-name-anchored: the leftmost qualifier resolves as a library type in the
// referencing file (e.g. `import java.util.Map; Map.Entry`).
type_ref_resolves("lib", ref, inner) :- java_type_reference(_, _, owner, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    lib_member_chain(anchor, ctn, inner, _),
    lib_type(anchorName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, anchor),
    java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, fp, _, _, _, _, _, owner),
    type_resolves_in_file("lib", anchorName, fp, anchor).

// Fully-qualified: completeTypeName is the full library path (`java.util.Map.Entry`).
type_ref_resolves("lib", ref, resolved) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    lib_nested_fqn(resolved, ctn, _).

// ── Inline fully-qualified library reference ────────────────────────────────
// A top-level type written with its full package path inline and no import
// (e.g. "java.util.Date date"): completeTypeName is the full qualifiedName. Match
// it directly against a library type's qualifiedName — precise, no simple-name
// explosion across libraries. TOP_LEVEL only: a library IR may still flatten a
// nested type's qualifiedName (Arrays.ArrayList registered as java.util.ArrayList,
// same as the real top-level java.util.ArrayList), and without this filter the
// inline FQN "java.util.ArrayList" over-resolves to BOTH. A flattened qname can only
// equal a written FQN by collision, so filtering to TOP_LEVEL drops exactly those
// collisions. An inline FQN of a nested library type resolves through lib_nested_fqn
// above, which walks the nesting and never reads the flattened string.
type_ref_resolves("lib", ref, resolved) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    lib_type(tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, "TOP_LEVEL_PLACEMENT", _, _, _, _, _, _, resolved).

// Inline fully-qualified CLIENT reference (e.g. "org.keycloak.X.TokenManager t"):
// a client type written by its full package path with no import. Mirrors the lib
// rule above, for any placement: a client nested type's qualifiedName is its full
// chain, so "a.b.Outer.Inner" written inline equals it (the member walk above finds
// the same type; the hash is shared, so nothing doubles). Without this, the
// qualified-ref guard leaves FQN client refs unresolved.
type_ref_resolves("client", ref, resolved) :- java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ref),
    ctn != tn,
    java_type(tn, ctn, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, resolved).
